// SOUNDNESS (BUG — this test is RED): an out-binding `(x:)` on a call must NOT silently re-declare a name that
// the enclosing map already bound in its write-back list `(x)`. Today it does: `(x:)` introduces a fresh local
// that SHADOWS the queried column, so the call's result lands on the throwaway shadow and the column is never
// written — it keeps its stale value. Like `map_self_binder_unqueried`, this should be a hard semantic error
// (or, at least, `(x:)` on an already-bound name should write the queried column). For arrays the bug hides
// (the shadow aliases the column's storage, so the write "works"); on a scalar it is plainly observable.
//
// `mark` is a value-producing effect: `sys_write` returns its byte count (3), written to stderr so it can't
// reach the checked stdout. `mark()(k:)` shadows the queried `k`, so `k` stays 0 and the program prints `k=0`.
// This test asserts the write reaches the column (`k=3`); it fails until the shadowing is rejected/fixed.
// RUN: %arche build -o %t %s && %t 2>/dev/null | grep -qx "k=3"
#import { fmt os }

mark :: func() -> Eff(i64) {
  return os.sys_write(2, "..\n", 3);
}

k :: i64;
B :: arche { k }
[1]B(1);

seed :: system {
  B.k = { 0 };
}

// `k:` re-declares a NEW `k` shadowing the map's queried + write-bound `k`; mark's 3 lands on the shadow, so
// the queried column is never written and keeps 0.
bad :: system eff {
  map (query { k })(k) eff {
    mark()(k:);
  };
}

show :: system eff {
  map (query { k }) eff {
    fmt.printf("k=%d\n", k);
  };
}

#run seq({ seed, bad, show })
